  "Number": 10,
  "Question": "The number of revolutions of a wheel, with fixed center and with an outside diameter of \\(6\\) feet, required to cause a point on the rim to go one mile is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(880\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{440}{\\pi}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{880}{\\pi}\\)",
    "D": "\\(440\\pi\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Because the center is fixed, the rim point travels along a circle of diameter 6 feet. In one revolution it travels one circumference, which is\n\\[\n\\pi\\cdot6=6\\pi\\text{ feet}.\n\\]\nOne mile is 5280 feet. The number of revolutions is the total distance divided by the distance per revolution:\n\\[\n\\frac{5280}{6\\pi}=\\frac{880}{\\pi}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(880/\\pi\\) revolutions, choice C.",
